NOTE: opening this port resets the Arduino and de-energizes the
      capper electromagnet. Do not run while a cap is held.
boot banner after  3.80s: 'OK:Ready'
  STATUS (read-only)       dt=   0.01s  reply=OK:{"homed":0,"pos":0.00,"max_vol":300.00}
      firmware max_vol = 300.0 uL -- must match pipette_model in the gantry YAML

Commanding plunger motion. Watch the plunger, not the screen.
Bounded to +3 mm of forward travel, then asked for it back.

WHAT THE TIMINGS MEAN: stepMotor() in the PANDA firmware bit-bangs
the STEP pin and counts, with no feedback of any kind. A round trip
that scales at ~0.673 s/mm proves the ARDUINO emitted the steps -- it
says nothing about whether the motor turned. Only your eyes can tell
you that. A flat ~0.1 s means the firmware declined to step at all.

  -- HOME --
  HOME (cold)              dt=   0.52s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette homed"}                   pos=0.0 homed=1
  -- forward: does distance scale? --
  MOVE_TO 1.0 mm           dt=   0.68s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette moved","v":[1.00]}        pos=1.0 homed=1
  MOVE_TO 3.0 mm           dt=   1.35s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette moved","v":[3.00]}        pos=3.0 homed=1
  -- HOME from a non-zero position: does it retract? --
  HOME (from 3.0)          dt=   0.52s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette homed"}                   pos=0.0 homed=1
  -- backward: the same positions, asked for in reverse --
  MOVE_TO 3.0 mm           dt=   2.02s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette moved","v":[3.00]}        pos=3.0 homed=1
  MOVE_TO 1.0 mm (back)    dt=   0.11s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette moved","v":[1.00]}        pos=3.0 homed=1
  MOVE_TO 0.0 mm (back)    dt=   0.11s  reply=OK:{"msg":"Pipette moved"}                   pos=3.0 homed=1

VERDICT: the limit switch is reading ASSERTED (D9 HIGH).
  HOME returned in 0.52s -- that is only the 796-step
  back-off, no seek -- and every UP move was refused in
  ~0.11s (1 step + the 100 ms debounce, then abort).
  Both come from the same read: stepMotor() aborts when the
  switch is HIGH and DIR is LOW, and homePipette() calls it
  an instant success. This is NOT a DIR fault.

  D9 is INPUT_PULLUP and HIGH means 'at the limit', so an
  open switch circuit reads asserted. Check, in order:
    1. Pipette pin 6 -> Arduino GND (the switch return). The
       Cubware diagram labels it but draws no wire.
    2. Pipette pin 7 -> Arduino D9 (the switch signal).
    3. The contact must be NORMALLY CLOSED: LOW at rest,
       opening as the plunger reaches the limit.

NOTE: forward moves DID emit steps (0.68s for 1 mm). That
  proves the Arduino toggled STEP -- it says nothing about
  whether the motor turned. If you saw and heard nothing, the
  fault is downstream of the Arduino, in order of likelihood:
    1. EN is not held LOW. The firmware's ENABLE is Arduino A4;
       the Cubware diagram wires the driver's EN to A3, which the
       firmware drives as DIR. Move that wire to A4.
    2. No motor supply on the driver's +/- terminal (12 V). VDD
       from the Arduino powers the logic only; STEP/DIR are
       accepted silently with no coil current.
    3. VREF potentiometer at zero. UART never configures the
       current (firmware transmits on A1, diagram wires A0), so
       the pot is the only thing setting it.
  Total silence (no buzz, no holding torque) means no coil
  current at all. A mixed-up coil pair buzzes instead.
